MODULE ConstraintChecks;

REQUIRE TestUtils;

// a property a constraint is put on (CONSTRAINT p(a) ...) keeps which changes are checked against it : a type and, for
// CHECKED BY, the properties. Modules that do not require each other are parsed at once, each in a thread of its own,
// so two of them that put constraints on one property of another module wrote the two at once, and the type of one
// could be left with the properties of the other - CHECKED BY with none, which failed the start. And which of the two
// constraints decided was the one written last, so the parse threads' timing : it is the one of the module later in the
// module order now, the pair NF at its module's version, as when the modules were parsed one after the other. The NF
// property is made with the property; writing the check takes only the NF property's monitor, without locking the property.
// The module-order test writes to a property made at runtime : a started one's checks are final

ccName = DATA LOCAL STRING ();
ccNegative () = ccName() == 'negative';

ccOrderFailure = DATA LOCAL STRING ();
ccCheckInModuleOrder INTERNAL 'lsfusion.tests.SetConstraintChecksInModuleOrderAction' ();

testConstraintChecksInModuleOrder 'Of two modules putting constraints on a property the later one in the module order decides what is checked' () {
    ccCheckInModuleOrder();
    assertNull(ccOrderFailure(), ccOrderFailure());
}
